# Partner / White-Label Architecture

This game supports multiple white-label partners (Ember, GamerSaloon, Sparket, Kaboom, FreeWin, BlockHead, Butr, MidoLotto, Speed, Toast, US). Each partner has different behavior for game-end flow, lobby layout, currency display, asset themes, and navigation.

Currently there are **77+ `if (CURRENT_PARTNER === PARTNER_ID.xx)` branches** scattered across 19+ files. This is unmaintainable — every new partner requires modifying dozens of existing files.

---

## The Strategy Pattern

All partner-varying behavior must be encapsulated behind a `PartnerBehavior` interface. Each partner provides one implementation. Shared code never checks `CURRENT_PARTNER` directly.

### Step 1: Define the Interface

```ts
// partners/PartnerBehavior.ts
export interface PartnerBehavior {
  readonly partnerId: string;

  // Game lifecycle
  onGameEnd(context: GameEndContext): void;
  onMatchResult(context: MatchResultContext): void;
  shouldShowRematch(): boolean;
  shouldShowLeaderboard(): boolean;

  // Navigation
  getPostGameAction(): "lobby" | "disconnect" | "result";
  getLobbyScreenClass(): typeof Container;

  // Currency
  mapCurrencyCode(code: string): string;
  getDefaultCurrency(): string;

  // Assets & theming
  getAssetBundleSuffix(): string;
  getDefaultAvatarUrl(): string;

  // Feature flags
  supportsWallet(): boolean;
  supportsPracticeMode(): boolean;
  supportsRequeue(): boolean;
}
```

### Step 2: Default Implementation

```ts
// partners/DefaultPartnerBehavior.ts
export class DefaultPartnerBehavior implements PartnerBehavior {
  readonly partnerId: string;

  constructor(partnerId: string, private config: PartnerConfig) {
    this.partnerId = partnerId;
  }

  onGameEnd(context: GameEndContext): void {
    context.navigation.goBackToLobby(true);
  }

  shouldShowRematch(): boolean { return true; }
  shouldShowLeaderboard(): boolean { return false; }
  getPostGameAction(): "lobby" | "disconnect" | "result" { return "lobby"; }
  mapCurrencyCode(code: string): string { return code; }

  // ... sensible defaults for all methods
}
```

### Step 3: Partner-Specific Overrides (Only What Differs)

```ts
// partners/EmberPartnerBehavior.ts
export class EmberPartnerBehavior extends DefaultPartnerBehavior {
  mapCurrencyCode(code: string): string {
    if (code === "EMT") return CURRENCY_CODES.PLAY_COINS;
    if (code === "SATS") return CURRENCY_CODES.PLAY_CASH;
    return code;
  }

  getLobbyScreenClass() { return EmberLobby; }
  supportsWallet(): boolean { return true; }
}
```

```ts
// partners/ButrPartnerBehavior.ts
export class ButrPartnerBehavior extends DefaultPartnerBehavior {
  onGameEnd(context: GameEndContext): void {
    context.socketManager.disconnect();
    // Butr disconnects instead of navigating back
  }

  getPostGameAction(): "disconnect" { return "disconnect"; }
  shouldShowRematch(): boolean { return false; }
}
```

### Step 4: Registry and Factory

```ts
// partners/partnerRegistry.ts
const partnerBehaviors = new Map<string, PartnerBehavior>();

export function registerPartner(behavior: PartnerBehavior) {
  partnerBehaviors.set(behavior.partnerId, behavior);
}

export function getPartnerBehavior(partnerId: string): PartnerBehavior {
  const behavior = partnerBehaviors.get(partnerId);
  if (!behavior) {
    throw new Error(`No behavior registered for partner: ${partnerId}`);
  }
  return behavior;
}

// Register all at app startup
registerPartner(new DefaultPartnerBehavior(PARTNER_ID.ts, PARTNER_SPECIFIC_CONFIG[PARTNER_ID.ts]));
registerPartner(new EmberPartnerBehavior(PARTNER_ID.em, PARTNER_SPECIFIC_CONFIG[PARTNER_ID.em]));
registerPartner(new ButrPartnerBehavior(PARTNER_ID.bt, PARTNER_SPECIFIC_CONFIG[PARTNER_ID.bt]));
// ... etc
```

### Step 5: Usage in Shared Code

```ts
// GOOD — partner behavior is polymorphic, no branching in shared code
class GameScreen extends Container {
  private partner: PartnerBehavior;

  constructor(deps: GameScreenDeps) {
    super();
    this.partner = deps.partnerBehavior;
  }

  endGameHandler(result: GameResult) {
    this.partner.onGameEnd({ result, navigation: this.deps.navigator });
  }
}
```

---

## Rules

### Never Add `CURRENT_PARTNER ===` Checks to Shared Code

This is the most important rule. **Zero tolerance.** If you need partner-specific behavior:

1. Add a method to `PartnerBehavior` interface
2. Implement default in `DefaultPartnerBehavior`
3. Override in the specific partner class
4. Call through the interface in shared code

### `PARTNER_SPECIFIC_CONFIG` Remains for Static Data

The existing config object in `network/constants.ts` is fine for static configuration (URLs, feature flags, display names). But **behavioral branching** (what happens on game end, how navigation flows, how currencies map) must go through the Strategy pattern, not `if/else` chains reading the config.

```ts
// Static config — OK
const config = PARTNER_SPECIFIC_CONFIG[CURRENT_PARTNER];
const apiUrl = config.apiBaseUrl;
const showWallet = config.features.wallet;

// Behavioral branching — must use Strategy
// BAD:  if (CURRENT_PARTNER === PARTNER_ID.em) { mapCurrency(...); }
// GOOD: partnerBehavior.mapCurrencyCode(code);
```

### Lobby Factory Pattern Is Correct — Extend It

The existing `lobbyScreenFactory.ts` already selects the right lobby screen class per partner. This is the right pattern. Extend it to all partner-varying concerns:

- Game end behavior
- Result screen layout
- Currency display logic
- Requeue/rematch availability
- Post-game navigation

### New Partner Checklist

Adding a new partner should require ONLY:

1. Create `partners/NewPartnerBehavior.ts` extending `DefaultPartnerBehavior`
2. Override only the methods that differ from default
3. Add one line to `partnerRegistry.ts` to register it
4. Add static config to `PARTNER_SPECIFIC_CONFIG`
5. Add raw assets to `raw-assets-newpartner/`

It must NOT require modifying `GameScreen`, `TournamentGameScreen`, `PhaseOne`, `PhaseTwo`, `ResultScreen`, `Navigation`, or any other shared file.

